WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.459
Good morning John; I love Tumblr text posts.

00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:07.209
As they rise to the top of the most massive
slush pile in the history of the universe

00:00:07.209 --> 00:00:09.720
getting six or even seven figures of notes.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.230
Accumulating the endorsement of seemingly
everyone, they become almost objectively great.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.190
Like no one could argue with their greatness.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.701
And when I read them, I usually agree.

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:23.360
A nugget of insight, a fascinating explanation,
a truly daring set of puns.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.810
Most text posts that break out into virality
are the height of a form -- a pretty weird

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:32.050
form, agreed, but I also like the Tumblr text
post because, like all media, they say a lot

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.190
about the culture that creates and honors
and promotes them.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.100
And here's one that I came across quite a
while ago that kinda stuck with me, “What

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.239
I’m really terrified of is leading an average,
ordinary life with a regular job and an invariable

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:50.550
routine, planned holidays, an average household,
fixed responsibilities and not doing anything

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.760
different to be remembered by.”

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.140
This post has around half a million notes,
and that makes me a little worried because

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.949
John, as you have, for some reason, repeatedly
driven home over and over again, probably

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:05.930
because you struggle with this quite a lot
yourself, oblivion is inevitable and someday

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.210
there will be no one left to remember anything,
let alone you or me.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.050
Being remembered forever is not a good goal
because it is not an achievable goal.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.260
But if that argument doesn’t work for you,
take a look at me.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.430
I am what people call successful; I've had
more than my share of interesting life experiences.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.030
I've toured in a rock band, been written about
in the New Yorker, helped raise over 2 million

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.550
dollars for charity, Crash Course is watched
in thousands of schools, and I don’t know

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.470
if you heard about this, but recently I interviewed
the President.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.890
So you might ask, when was it for me when
I knew that I had made it?

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:38.030
When was the moment when I had the success
statue and I got to put it on this shelf right

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.030
here next to Obi-Wan?

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.540
Was it when I was first recognized in public
or when we got one hundred thousand subscribers

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.320
or a million subscribers or when I was first
paying my bills with YouTube?

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.410
Which day was it when I became perpetually
satisfied and always happy?

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:49.810
Yeah, it was none of the days.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.920
Here’s the thing about success, if you're
smart enough and you're dedicated enough and

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.930
lucky enough and sacrifice enough, you too
will get to find out that none of it exists.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:58.930
It’s made up.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.820
It’s just it’s not a real thing.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.730
You get successes, but you don’t get success.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:06.680
The whole of everything of it does not lie
on that arbitrary yet remarkable thing that

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.009
you did that everyone says, “Yes, that.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:10.009
That counts.”

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.450
I've had those things, and they do not bring
long term satisfaction.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.469
The satisfaction?

00:02:13.469 --> 00:02:14.579
The joy?

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:16.620
That comes from solving problems and making
things.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.269
And often times the recognition isn’t even
a necessary part of the process.

00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:22.969
So if I have any advice for the five-hundred-thousand
people who either liked or reblogged that

00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:27.420
text post on Tumblr, here it is: instead of
thinking about what you will and won’t accomplish

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.160
in your life, think about the problems that
you have helped solve and you will help solve

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.920
and the things that you're gonna help make
and that you've already helped make.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.069
I don’t think I am alone in feeling like
the things that we help make and the problems

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:43.030
we help solve, those are the things that really
matter the things that actually affect the

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.530
world and that actually make us happy.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:49.950
And maybe every person is unremarkable in
that we all make things every day.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.469
We make happiness and we make families and
we make sandwiches and most fantastically

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:56.159
of all we make ourselves.

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:58.319
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:02:59.319
End screen.

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:00.989
And you know why we love video games so much?

00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:07.310
Because they give us clear defined problems
to solve and value-laden achievements to unlock,

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:13.140
and I love it, which is why Games with Hank
officially today is back with new series “Hank

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.280
Green Plays The Sims.”

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:20.920
And then of course you can adjust booby size
to the beat of the music.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:26.010
Meet Jimmy Carter, he is like ninety years
old, so let's go ahead and make him an old

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.010
man.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.010
Oh good.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.010
Sul sul Jimmy Carter.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.269
I mean I guess we could give him emo hair.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:31.269
Hahaha.

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:33.999
Oh no, no, what have I done!

00:03:33.999 --> 00:03:34.999
Ohhhhh God!

00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:36.279
You look great.

00:03:36.279 --> 00:03:37.609
You're in great shape, Jimmy.

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:38.709
Leader of the free world?

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:41.290
That’s not a lifetime wish, Jimmy Carter.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.649
You already got to do that.

